-- title:
--   Norming functionals for pairs of dense points
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. Let $(z_j)$ be a dense sequence in $K$.
--
--   1. **Pair norming functionals.** For each pair $(i,j)$, a functional $\varphi_{ij}\in X^*$ with
--   $$
--   \|\varphi_{ij}\|\le1,\qquad\varphi_{ij}(z_i-z_j)=\|z_i-z_j\| .
--   $$
--   These countably many functionals separate the points of $K$. Their evaluations embed the weakly compact set $K$ into a countable product of lines, so the weak topology on $K$ is metrizable without separability of $X$ or $X^*$.
